WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.632 --> 00:00:03.771
(thunder strike)

00:00:03.771 --> 00:00:06.271
(banjo music)

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:08.873
- What do you think of

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:09.983
when you hear the word, 'banjo'?

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.990
Maybe you think of an iconic Muppet frog

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.810
strumming the first notes
of "Rainbow Connection,"

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.310
or even footage of a certain 1970s comic

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.350
using it with his routine.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.190
The banjo was at one time

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.590
the most popular instrument in America

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:26.720
with tens of thousands

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.550
manufactured and sold
between 1910 and 1930 alone.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.700
It's been called America's instrument.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.570
But how it became a Bluegrass staple

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.700
started in a place many don't know about.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.350
I flew out to Nashville
to meet some of the

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.320
best banjo players in the world,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.010
and hopefully I can talk one of them

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.210
into giving me a banjo lesson.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.728
- Well, there's not much to it.

00:00:46.728 --> 00:00:49.545
♪ Baby shark, do do do do do do ♪

00:00:49.545 --> 00:00:51.908
♪ Baby shark do do do do do do ♪

00:00:51.908 --> 00:00:54.239
♪ Baby shark do do do do do do ♪

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:56.410
♪ Baby shark ♪

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.572
♪ Mama ♪

00:00:57.572 --> 00:00:58.405
Oh come on, Dante.

00:00:58.405 --> 00:00:59.405
You know it.

00:01:01.423 --> 00:01:04.007
♪ I was born unto this earth ♪

00:01:04.007 --> 00:01:07.721
♪ Green grasses softened my birth ♪

00:01:07.721 --> 00:01:10.874
♪ Blue heaven shuttered me deep ♪

00:01:10.874 --> 00:01:15.874
♪ Beneath an evergreen sky ♪

00:01:15.999 --> 00:01:19.070
♪ I withdrew from the light of the sun ♪

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.770
- We want to know more about the banjo

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.780
as an instrument, the history behind it,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.970
just all things about the banjo.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.490
'Cause we think that the banjo

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.115
deserves a little more attention.

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:31.948
- Agreed.

00:01:31.948 --> 00:01:32.781
- Agreed, yeah.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.754
♪ She was a rose ♪

00:01:36.754 --> 00:01:39.133
♪ A blooming rose ♪

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:41.160
- [Nahre] Bela Fleck and Abigail Washburn

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.980
both play banjo.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.459
Correction, they are both banjo masters.

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:49.780
(banjo strums)

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.220
Bela has been dubbed the
world's premier banjo player

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.410
and has performed solo and
with his band The Flecktones.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.110
His unique sound called Blu-bop,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.790
a mix of bluegrass and jazz

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.663
has led to him winning fourteen Grammy's.

00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:04.901
♪ At first it was only a trace ♪

00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:08.120
♪ And the beauty quickened its pace ♪

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.880
- [Nahre] Abigail, a
Grammy winner as well,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.131
performs as a soloist
and with old time bands

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:13.850
to international acclaim.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.750
She works different cultures
into her performances.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.012
She also performs with
her husband, Bela Fleck.

00:02:20.012 --> 00:02:23.716
♪ And the towers rise up on the bough ♪

00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:26.240
♪ 'Till there is nothing left we can do ♪

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.133
- [Nahre] Abigail and Bela
play very distinctive styles.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.730
While their instruments
look similar to one another,

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.820
a closer look at their hands shows

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.280
how they create very different sounds.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.320
A typical bluegrass style
is three finger picking,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.750
using picks on the thumb,
index, and middle fingers.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.214
It's almost like you need
three hands to do it.

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:48.170
(rapid banjo strumming)

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.900
- So, in other words, with a flat pick

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.610
you'd hit the same string
over and over again.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:53.460
Like up and down.

00:02:54.294 --> 00:02:55.429
(banjo strums)

00:02:55.429 --> 00:02:56.530
But with these...

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.863
(rapid banjo strumming)

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.410
- This is like a roll that's just going

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:04.860
straight along.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.263
So we're playing 16th notes.

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:09.650
We're playing 1 e &amp; a 2 e &amp; a.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.690
Now, here's the fun part.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.080
When you do a forward
roll, which is like this.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.800
(banjo strums)

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.633
So see I'm going

00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:20.530
a 1 e &amp; a 2 e &amp; a 3 e &amp; a 4 e &amp; a.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.400
You see I'm playing 16th note but look,

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.072
I'm doing it with three fingers.

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:28.230
- [Nahre] It goes from one to two.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.710
- One has to come up
on a different finger,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.060
like one, two, three, four.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.850
One, two, three, four.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.750
So the rhythm, you kinda have to feel that

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.620
rhythm as you're going along.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:42.210
When you get that feel for it
then you can make it happen.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.753
- So try this, can you do this?

00:03:43.753 --> 00:03:46.336
(banjo strums)

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.750
So this is the tricky thing that

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.460
the highest string is
next to the lowest string.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.680
It's not where you expect
it in your mind to be.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.367
Okay, now try this real slow

00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:58.950
(banjo strums)

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:04.673
Great.

00:04:04.673 --> 00:04:07.135
(banjo strums)

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.090
That's it.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.690
Now repeat those for an hour and a half.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.530
- So, now Nahre, you're going

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:22.130
to learn the better form of banjo playing.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.348
- Okay.

00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:25.590
- Just in case you were wondering.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.290
- [Nahre] Another style is clawhammer

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.589
using the finger nails to
brush across the strings

00:04:29.589 --> 00:04:33.380
as the thumb drops and picks
directly on the strings.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.910
- It's really like a strike of one string

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.840
with either your pointer
or your middle finger.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.660
And then a brush, and then a

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.110
pull off on the drone string,

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.413
and that's good and all.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.400
But I got excited about
some other kinds of playing.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.320
One piece of clawhammer frailing

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.200
is that you can actually
drop your thumb down.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.422
So, this is another...

00:04:56.422 --> 00:04:59.005
(banjo strums)

00:05:05.915 --> 00:05:08.370
(banjo strums rapidly)

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.710
So that's just striking down.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.700
So he's pulling up with his picks,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:12.880
with his metal picks.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.556
And I'm striking down
with my finger nails.

00:05:15.556 --> 00:05:18.139
(banjo strums)

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.314
Do that for an hour and a half.

00:05:22.314 --> 00:05:23.786
(laughing)

00:05:23.786 --> 00:05:25.450
- An hour and a half.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:26.330
- Oh, this is pretty good,

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:27.240
when he was courting me

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.440
he tried to learn my
style of banjo playing.

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:32.364
It was funny.

00:05:33.657 --> 00:05:35.790
- I wasn't very good.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.060
- We can see how one instrument

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.200
proves to be a facet of sounds.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.270
But where did these sounds originate?

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.280
While it's been called
a America's instrument

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.223
the banjo's ancestry like
most of American culture

00:05:46.223 --> 00:05:49.363
can actually be traced to
many different countries.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.160
Drum-like instruments with strings

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.020
are recorded in the history of several

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.700
far East, Middle Eastern, Mediterranean,

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.870
and African civilizations.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.500
This idea of the banjo was brought

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.620
to America from the enslaved Africans

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.570
in the 17th Century.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:06.520
These early banjos were often made

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.890
out of gourds, wood, turtle shells,

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:12.150
or tree trunks with tanned
animal skin wrapped over them.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.210
- When Bela went to Africa it
was really exciting for me.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.090
I saw his documentary afterwards.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.020
And he was in the Gambia

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.520
and playing with a family
called the Jatta family.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.260
And they have these instruments
called the akonting.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.230
They're three string.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:27.730
And they're basically this banjo,

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.870
but they're three string
and they're made with gourd.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.550
And the way they played just looks

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.111
exactly the way I play.

00:06:33.111 --> 00:06:35.436
It just looks exactly like it.

00:06:35.436 --> 00:06:36.700
- [Bela] The right hand
is exactly the same.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.533
- Yeah.

00:06:37.533 --> 00:06:39.750
- In the early years of
its American evolution,

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.320
the banjo would be
considered a folk instrument

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.140
played mostly by and for enslaved people.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.320
By the mid-1800s, the banjo gained

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.650
international popularity
with traveling minstrels.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.795
A racist and demeaning
form of entertainment

00:06:52.795 --> 00:06:55.840
where white performers
dressed in black face,

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:57.650
and often used the banjo.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.970
Recreating a distorted
view of slavery in America

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.420
as a care-free, happy existence.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.460
One of those minstrel players
was Joel Walker Sweeney.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.180
Credited with popularizing
the five string banjo,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.060
Sweeney contracted a
drum maker in Baltimore,

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.670
William Boucher Jr.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:16.560
to manufacture banjos for public sale.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.310
Boucher's introduction
of metal-tuning brackets

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.630
to tighten the head was incorporated into

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.740
what is known as the "Boucher Banjo."

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.850
This was a major step
in the banjo's journey

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.350
from being a home-made, folk creation

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.933
to the instrument we see today.

00:07:33.805 --> 00:07:36.388
(banjo strums)

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.051
- I have made, since 2000, we've done

00:07:44.051 --> 00:07:48.352
about 18 hundred and 25 banjos
so far, that I've built.

00:07:48.352 --> 00:07:50.740
(banjo strums)

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.030
So a banjo's basically auto parts.

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.160
That's what I like.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:54.993
- Auto parts?

00:07:54.993 --> 00:07:56.410
- Yeah, not really, but
you know what I mean.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.460
It's metal, okay?

00:07:57.460 --> 00:07:59.337
A guitar is mainly it's all wood.

00:07:59.337 --> 00:08:01.837
(banjo music)

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:05.620
A banjo you can take apart.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.830
So you've gotta to imagine about

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:08.840
75 parts to a banjo if you're

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.120
counting every little tiny
thing, or something like that.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.972
On a guitar, mandolin,
you're forming the wood.

00:08:13.972 --> 00:08:15.390
You're gluing the braces.

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.520
You're gluing the neck, and
you're gluing the bridge on.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.180
You really can't take that apart.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.720
So, completely different.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.460
That's why I like to say it's auto parts.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.350
And you can change the
thickness of that head,

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.160
and get a completely different sound.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.543
The only other instrument
that uses that is a drum.

00:08:28.543 --> 00:08:29.490
Right?

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.643
So a banjo's basically a
drum with a neck on it.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.610
Hey, how'd you like that?

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:38.800
- Beautiful!

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.300
By the end of the Civil War the

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.580
banjo spread across players of all

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.060
different kinds of backgrounds.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.880
After the Civil War, the banjo was

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.280
considered legitimatized and
used for classical performance,

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.231
as well as a new popular sound, ragtime.

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:55.187
(ragtime music)

00:08:55.187 --> 00:08:57.160
- [Steve] It used to be
the turn of the century

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.580
there were these five string,

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.004
they were actually five string banjos.

00:09:00.004 --> 00:09:02.690
The guys were playing
classical music on them,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.290
the most gorgeous music.

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.283
And they say up in Boston,

00:09:06.283 --> 00:09:09.520
there was a banjo on every corner.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.610
- The banjo was it in America.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:15.570
Banjo players were just as
popular as today's pop stars.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.307
But then the Great Depression hit.

00:09:17.307 --> 00:09:20.800
(instrumental music)

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.260
By 1940, for practical
purposes the banjo was dead.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.190
Guitars sold for one-third
of the price of a banjo,

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:31.453
and thus, took its place
as big band music took off.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.860
I'd love to hear
anything, done by you two.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.837
- Okay, we've got a tune
that we've been working on.

00:09:38.837 --> 00:09:41.337
(banjo music)

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:58.630
- [Nahre] Five string folk banjo

00:09:58.630 --> 00:09:59.960
was bubbling behind the scenes

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.220
in Appalachia and the Ozarks.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.210
Artists like Pete Seeger introduced

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:07.240
the instrument to the masses
as the folk boom launched.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:10.157
(slow banjo music)

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.500
This was also known as the folk scare,

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.470
for political stances taken by several

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.040
musicians viewed as rebels.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:20.010
And a new identity was solidified

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.380
in the banjo as artists
like, Earl Scruggs,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.510
popularized the three finger picking style

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.437
that re-defined the instrument.

00:10:26.437 --> 00:10:30.190
(lively banjo music)

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:31.908
- Bela Fleck said it, he said,

00:10:31.908 --> 00:10:34.230
"We all wanted to sound like Earl."

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:38.010
- By the 1960s and 70s
the banjo was locked in

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.241
with country and bluegrass music.

00:10:40.241 --> 00:10:42.741
(banjo music)

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:48.720
So do you think it's just a matter of,

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.020
more people being exposed to the banjo

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:54.030
for them to gravitate
towards it as an instrument?

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.000
- Absolutely.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.130
There used to be this paradigm
about banjos just kind of,

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:02.400
country, hay bales kind of thing.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:03.453
But that's gone.

00:11:04.351 --> 00:11:06.851
(banjo music)

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.090
So my theory is

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.349
players like Bela,

00:11:17.349 --> 00:11:19.530
they're aliens.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.624
Mark my words, they're aliens.

00:11:21.624 --> 00:11:23.080
It's true.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.373
Aliens are here.

00:11:24.373 --> 00:11:26.873
(banjo music)

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:37.500
- [Nahre] Today, banjo
has had another rebirth.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.380
With bands like Trampled by Turtles,

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.110
and Mumford and Sons,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.983
bringing the sound to new audiences.

00:11:44.641 --> 00:11:47.940
(lively banjo music)

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:52.320
- I've seen a change in
bluegrass music, recently.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.779
A lot of younger folks getting into it.

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:56.660
They're playing for two years, right?

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.450
And they're six times better than I was

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.270
when I was playing for eight years.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:02.170
It's amazing.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:03.003
Yeah.

00:12:03.003 --> 00:12:04.590
- But they can't make a banjo like you.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.050
- They probably can if they want to.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:08.320
(laughing)

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:09.803
- I wouldn't put it past them.

00:12:09.803 --> 00:12:12.970
(melodic banjo music)

00:12:16.448 --> 00:12:17.852
- I'm Dante Limon.

00:12:17.852 --> 00:12:19.830
I've been playing the banjo for

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.160
a little over a year.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.293
- [Nahre] What's your favorite
thing about the banjo?

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.170
- Well, the way it sounds, I guess.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.010
And how much fun it is to play it.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:31.180
- [Nahre] The instrument can be heard in

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.150
different genres from folk to pop.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:34.670
But then there's Rhiannon Giddens,

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:37.320
who returns to the real
origins of the instrument.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.930
Playing a Boucher-style banjo,

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:40.860
and sharing her beautiful, modern takes

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.627
on music from those early humble days.

00:12:43.627 --> 00:12:45.562
♪ A little bird, it strikes you now ♪

00:12:45.562 --> 00:12:48.856
♪ Through the shiver and shaking blue ♪

00:12:48.856 --> 00:12:51.203
♪ Don't know where I'm going, but ♪

00:12:51.203 --> 00:12:54.369
♪ I know what to do ♪

00:12:54.369 --> 00:12:57.536
(rhythmic soul music)

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.453
- There is a bigger banjo.

00:13:13.316 --> 00:13:15.983
(banjo strums)

00:13:17.091 --> 00:13:19.591
(banjo music)

00:13:24.425 --> 00:13:26.909
♪ One foot forward, and one foot back ♪

00:13:26.909 --> 00:13:29.144
♪ 20 time you've fall off the track ♪

00:13:29.144 --> 00:13:30.680
♪ One foot forward, and one foot back ♪

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:34.120
- It's got a sound no instrument
in the orchestra can make.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:35.430
And the banjo is one of those.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:36.550
It doesn't sound like a guitar.

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:37.820
It does not sound like a mandolin.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.670
It doesn't sound like a cello.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:40.911
It doesn't sound like anything else.

00:13:40.911 --> 00:13:43.830
And it's been fun to see
the change in my time

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:46.970
from being almost ostracized
for being a banjo player.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.247
But even in Nashville, which you think,

00:13:48.247 --> 00:13:49.090
"Oh, country music."

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:50.390
I moved to Nashville from New York

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.580
'cause there was some of a
bluegrass and a country scene,

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.137
And, "Oh, banjo's too country to have

00:13:55.137 --> 00:13:56.350
"on country records anymore."

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:57.778
So in the 80s it was a stigma,

00:13:57.778 --> 00:13:59.960
and you couldn't really
work as a banjo player.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:00.810
It would be very rare.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:01.817
The banjo was always the problem.

00:14:01.817 --> 00:14:04.047
"Oh, you'll never make it
with that banjo in there.

00:14:04.047 --> 00:14:05.167
"Bela should play guitar.

00:14:05.167 --> 00:14:06.760
"Or you guys should get drums."

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.380
But, what's happened since then

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:10.110
to now is people are putting banjo

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:11.740
on to things to give it legitimacy,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:12.870
where it used to be--

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:13.950
- Authenticity, yeah.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:14.783
- Yeah.

00:14:14.783 --> 00:14:15.920
- A different kind of sound that--

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.290
- Now you see it on rock records

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:18.123
and indie records.

00:14:18.123 --> 00:14:20.040
- Like the indie, alt-pop, folk scene

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.480
that came along and got pretty excited

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.880
about the banjo, offering a different

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.458
kind of sound and aura.

00:14:26.458 --> 00:14:28.187
♪ Bring it down ♪

00:14:28.187 --> 00:14:29.928
♪ Don't let it bring you down ♪

00:14:29.928 --> 00:14:32.080
♪ Don't let it bring you down ♪

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:34.414
- [Nahre] Time will show us
where the banjo goes next.

00:14:34.414 --> 00:14:36.500
♪ Don't let it bring you down ♪

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:37.840
- [Nahre] One thing is certain,

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:41.298
it's definitely still alive, and ringing.

00:14:41.298 --> 00:14:42.718
- How are you doing?

00:14:42.718 --> 00:14:45.301
(banjo strums)

00:14:48.070 --> 00:14:49.470
- [Nahre] Song writer Harlan Howard

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:50.987
has said that, "Country music is

00:14:50.987 --> 00:14:53.200
"three chords and the truth."

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.140
Get to know the amazing music and artist

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.040
behind this uniquely American sound

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:59.337
in Ken Burns' new eight part documentary,

00:14:59.337 --> 00:15:00.320
"Country Music."

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:01.940
Tune in or stream the series starting

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:04.246
on Sunday, September 15th.

00:15:04.246 --> 00:15:07.362
(sweeping electronic music)

